WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.948 --> 00:02:13.650
[MUSIC - EDWARD ELGAR, "POMP AND
 CIRCUMSTANCE"]

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.220
PRESENTER: Ladies and
gentlemen, the guests of honor,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.629
the class of 1994.

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:23.124
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.930
GRAY: The corporation and the
faculty of the Massachusetts

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:08.260
Institute of Technology
are now declared

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:12.070
convened together
with this assembly

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.770
on this glorious
morning in Cambridge

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.300
on the occasion of
the commencement

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:22.870
exercises of the Institute for
the conferring of its degrees.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.320
The stage assembly and
audience will please rise

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.330
and join Professor Ellen Harris,
associate provost for the arts,

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:35.470
in the singing of one verse
of "The Star-Spangled Banner,"

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:41.528
to be followed by the invocation
by the Reverent Scott Paradise.

00:04:41.528 --> 00:04:43.963
[MUSIC - "THE STAR SPANGLED
 BANNER"]

00:04:55.651 --> 00:04:58.640
HARRIS: [SINGING]
O, say, can you

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:06.103
see, by the dawn's early
light, what so proudly

00:05:06.103 --> 00:05:13.150
we hailed at the twilight's
last gleaming, whose

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:21.860
broad stripes and bright stars,
through the perilous fight o'er

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:30.555
the ramparts we watched
were so gallantly streaming?

00:05:30.555 --> 00:05:39.465
And the rocket's red glare,
the bombs bursting in air,

00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:49.100
gave proof through the night
that our flag was still there.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:06:03.051
O, say does that star-spangled
banner yet wave o'er

00:06:03.051 --> 00:06:18.955
the land of the free and
the home of the brave.

00:06:22.931 --> 00:06:24.919
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:32.134 --> 00:06:33.050
PARADISE: Let us pray.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.970
Oh, holy God, creator
of all things.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:45.930
We offer thanks with hearts
overflowing for the privilege

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:52.750
to live and work in this place,
for the opportunity to learn,

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.220
for the experiences
of testing, which

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:58.830
try us as silver is tried.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:05.050
For friends and mentors,
for the excitement of ideas,

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:06.150
the joy of discovery.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:12.650
Oh, God of justice and
love, we find the world

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:16.470
shaped not only by
human achievements,

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:17.855
but also by human failures.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:25.360
We have ended the Cold War and
reduced the nuclear threat.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.430
But we see violence proliferate
in families, on the streets,

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:36.880
in oppressive regimes, and in
civil wars around the globe.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.630
We have created great
wealth, but we have

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.130
acquiesced to great poverty.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:50.530
We have awakened as a nation
to the environmental crisis,

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:53.260
but we have been unwilling
to make the changes

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.970
necessary to resolve it.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.050
Oh, God, giver of life and
hope, we commit ourselves

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.075
to a renewed sense of vocation.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.100
May we realize that
even the smallest

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:17.870
deeds of love and justice and
generosity can change history.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:21.830
May we learn to risk speaking
the truth when evil is

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:24.645
being done and all are silent.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:30.460
May we work less on the
problems of the powerful

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:34.370
and the prosperous
and more on the needs

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:39.143
of the poorest, the weakest,
and the most vulnerable

00:08:39.143 --> 00:08:40.309
of our brothers and sisters.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:46.240
May we together blaze
a path toward a world

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:51.010
at peace with itself and
reconciled with the earth.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:09:00.600
Oh, God, our companion on
the way, be with us as we

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.185
go forth from this place.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:10.315
Give us hope for the future
and joy in our hearts.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:13.680
Amen.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:17.440
Please be seated.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.600
GRAY: I'm pleased to welcome
to the platform this morning

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:43.240
the honorable Kenneth E. Reeves,
mayor of the city of Cambridge.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:46.096
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:53.330
It is my privilege to
welcome his highness, The Aga

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:58.730
Khan, spiritual leader of
the Shia Ismalli Muslims.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:03.832
His highness The Aga Khan
will now present the address.

00:10:03.832 --> 00:10:06.272
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:23.180
KHAN: President Vest,
members of the corporation

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:26.420
of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology,

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:30.890
distinguished members of
the faculty, Mayor Reeves,

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:35.420
happy graduates,
even happier parents,

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:38.210
and others gathered
here today, I

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:42.170
am pleased and honored to
be with you this morning.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:47.150
MIT has shown a
standard of excellence

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:51.500
in education and research
that sets a benchmark

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:55.410
for universities everywhere.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.920
You who have been at
the Institute for years

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:01.880
may be excused if you
take this in stride.

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:05.840
But for me, coming here for the
first time in several years,

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:08.135
the energy of the
place is palpable.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.890
Actually, my reaction
is somewhat personal.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:19.460
When as a young man I began
to think about colleges,

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:24.170
MIT was my first choice.

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:27.800
In the end, I acceded to the
advice of my grandfather, who

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.146
favored Harvard.

00:11:30.146 --> 00:11:32.511
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:40.360
In fact, I didn't even apply
anywhere other than MIT.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:44.620
And MIT accepted me on
the basis of my grades.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:48.190
They didn't even ask me to
take an examination, which

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.970
is good, because I could hardly
understand written English.

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:58.130
When my grandfather told me I
shouldn't didn't enroll here,

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.950
I was devastated.

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:06.860
That really put the kibosh
on my plans to study science.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:10.855
You see, I learned
Harvard English.

00:12:10.855 --> 00:12:13.230
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:21.600
Education has been important
to my family for a long time.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.660
My forefathers founded
Al-Azhar University in Cairo

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:29.130
some 1,000 years ago at the
time of the Fatimid Caliphate

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:32.050
in Egypt.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:37.120
Discovery of knowledge
was seen by those founders

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:42.010
as an embodiment of
religious faith and faith,

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:44.890
as reinforced by knowledge
of workings of the creator's

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:45.610
physical world.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:54.090
The form of universities has
changed over those 1,000 years.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:58.170
But that reciprocity
between faith and knowledge

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.000
remains a source of strength.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:09.760
MIT has changed also
over its 130 years.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:12.970
This university was
initially designed

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:18.280
to meet the needs of society in
a newly industrialized world.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.320
As the world and
its needs evolved,

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:23.210
so has MIT's curriculum.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:29.960
Steadily, the emphasis on
social sciences and humanities

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:35.730
has expanded as the Institute
has recognized increasingly

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.970
that the range of
technologies that

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:42.420
are needed to solve
societal problems

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:45.930
goes far beyond
those of engineering

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:46.890
and natural sciences.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:53.520
The increased richness
of education results

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:56.460
in an increasingly
versatile set of graduates.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:05.140
As I look out over those
today who are here,

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:09.400
I see that MIT has
changed in other ways.

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:12.250
The great continents
of the world

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:14.647
are now represented
in your student body

00:14:14.647 --> 00:14:15.480
and in your faculty.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:22.820
So too are the great
religions of the world.

00:14:22.820 --> 00:14:27.020
MIT seems prepared to take
advantage of excellence

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:29.390
from all quarters.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:32.510
A fact that is sure to
reinforce the Institute's

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:33.280
future strength.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.100
When I was thinking
about the theme

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:42.130
that I should choose
for this talk,

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:45.550
I considered first that
commencements are occasions

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.820
to reflect on general
truths, truths

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:54.620
that will retain their validity
over the course of your lives

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:58.300
and over a wide range of
intellectual interests

00:14:58.300 --> 00:14:59.610
that you graduates embody.

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:04.900
But how is that
search for generality

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:09.700
to be squared with the very
particular point in time

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:13.460
that today represents?

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:19.280
You and I are here
in a real sense

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:24.980
only because 1994
finds MIT and the world

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:29.930
at distinctive stages
of their evolution.

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:33.500
Still, the particular
can provide insight

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.240
into the general.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:39.530
So my comments today will
draw on the particular

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:43.865
in the hope of saying something
of value about the general.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:50.870
I shall talk today
about encounters,

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:57.890
encounters when two people
meet or two particles or two

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:00.640
cultures.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:04.110
In that crucial
moment of interaction,

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:08.200
the results of an
encounter are determined.

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:12.610
In the simplest of encounters,
say, with two billiard balls,

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:18.470
the outcome is a predictable
result of position, velocity,

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:21.010
and mass.

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:26.770
But the encounters that interest
me most are not so simple.

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:31.670
In the encounters of
people and cultures,

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:37.700
much depends on the path that
each has taken to that point.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:42.760
These are not
stochastic processes.

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:46.000
The subjects have histories.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:52.330
The encounter has complexity
and rich dimensionality.

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:55.000
The result of an encounter
between two people

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.000
or between two
cultures is shaped

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.890
by the assumptions of each,
by their respective goals,

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:07.630
and perhaps, more directly
relevant to a university,

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:11.710
by the repertoire of responses
that each has learned.

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:18.017
Encounters, therefore, have
aspects of both the general

00:17:18.017 --> 00:17:18.725
and the specific.

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:27.520
What makes our current
time distinctive

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:32.280
are the new combinations
of people and cultures

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:35.070
that are participating
in these encounters.

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:43.520
Two ongoing social
and political changes

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:48.840
illustrate the reasons for
these new combinations.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:52.920
The first is the
collapse of communism

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:57.570
in the USSR and Eastern Europe.

00:17:57.570 --> 00:18:02.420
You graduates may feel that
you've been at MIT forever.

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:06.470
But it really is remarkable
that the overturning

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:09.890
of the communist world has
occurred since most of you

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:13.830
started your studies here.

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:17.820
You go out into a world
where the rules are

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.610
different from those that
held when you entered.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:26.400
Colonialism is moribund.

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:33.040
No longer is it enough
to decide whether one

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:40.380
is aligned with communism or
capitalism in a bipolar world.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:44.860
Now a full range of
complicated choices

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:48.580
is opened up to people in the
developed and the developing

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:49.090
world alike.

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:56.490
A massive break on
change has been released.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:06.480
The potential for creative
action, creative encounters,

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.190
is now much enhanced.

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:15.450
This change is a work
in progress, however.

00:19:15.450 --> 00:19:17.760
The potential of
the moment must be

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:21.330
seized, for
conditions for change

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:25.020
may not always be so propitious.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:28.740
There is the real possibility
that the Soviet Union

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:34.340
may reconstitute itself if
the social upheaval that

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:37.280
accompanies political
disintegration

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:41.030
and economic reform
is allowed to become

00:19:41.030 --> 00:19:45.450
less tolerable than the
strictures of a totalitarian

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:45.950
state.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:55.620
While these shocks reverberate
from the ex-communist bloc,

00:19:55.620 --> 00:20:00.190
profound changes of a
very different character

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:04.770
are to be found in
the Islamic world.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:10.440
Here the changes are in
both perception and reality.

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:12.930
And both of them are
works in progress, too.

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:17.810
The Islamic world
is remarkably poorly

00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:24.500
understood by the West,
almost terra incognita.

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:29.160
Even now, one sees
pervasive images in the West

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:32.630
that caricature Muslims
as either oil sheiks

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:34.700
or unruly fundamentalists.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:44.400
The Islamic world is, in fact,
a rich and changing tapestry

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:49.430
which the West would
do well to understand.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:51.530
The economic power
of the Islamic world

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:56.360
is increasing, not so much
because of Middle Eastern oil,

00:20:56.360 --> 00:21:00.290
but because of the rapid
growth of newly industrializing

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:02.820
countries like
Malaysia and Indonesia.

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:10.430
Its population is increasing and
already represents nearly one

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:14.200
quarter of the world's total.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:20.180
It is remarkably diverse,
ethnically, economically,

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:24.640
politically, and in
its interpretations

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:25.750
of its own faith.

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:29.880
The Muslim world
can no longer be

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:33.400
thought of as a subset
of the developing world.

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:38.790
Islam is well represented in the
United States, Canada, United

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:40.860
Kingdom, and Western Europe.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.110
And that presence is growing.

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:51.220
The religious diversity of Islam
is important and misunderstood

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:54.290
by most non-Muslims.

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:57.980
This is not the forum to go
into the multiple reasons

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:01.340
for this misunderstanding.

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:05.270
But for many in the
West, the first awareness

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:08.780
that there were two major
branches of Islam, Shia

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:15.770
and Sunni, only came with
the Iranian revolution.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:20.870
That represents a
superficiality of understanding

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:25.820
that would be as though we
Muslims only just learned

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:29.540
that there were two branches
of Christianity, Protestant

00:22:29.540 --> 00:22:33.860
and Catholic, and
had no understanding

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:39.830
of the Reformation, the
authority of the church,

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:42.260
or the ideas that led
to the proliferation

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:47.370
of Protestant sects in
the 16th and 17th century.

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:50.790
Or as though we thought
that most Americans were

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:52.020
Branch Davidians.

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:58.990
In the face of such
lack of knowledge,

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:03.010
about one quarter of
the world's population,

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:06.010
one may reasonably ask what
the role of the university

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:07.850
is in setting this straight.

00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:14.280
It seems clear to me that
at the most basic level,

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:18.460
the university is responsible
for helping its students

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:23.900
to learn not only the
simplifying principles

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:26.510
that the various learned
disciplines found

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:30.300
useful in understanding
our world,

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:36.170
but also the rich complexity
of history and language

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:40.730
and culture that make
real-life problems

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:45.040
interesting and difficult.

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:51.310
MIT now teaches both of these
lessons well and vigorously.

00:23:51.310 --> 00:23:55.450
But it seems not
always to have done so.

00:23:55.450 --> 00:24:01.780
Indeed, I am told that at
the opening of MIT in 1865,

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:06.880
one local newspaper reported
with a note of triumph

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:10.600
that the creation of
MIT, quote, "sealed

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:15.280
the fate of that system by
which our youth waste the best

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:19.570
portion of student life in
burrowing into the grammars

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:23.140
and dictionaries of races less
enlightened than their own,"

00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:25.600
unquote.

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:28.810
MIT has clearly come
around to thinking

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:34.400
that those less enlightened
races have something to teach.

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:37.540
And that teaching
helps the university

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:41.540
fulfill its potential.

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:45.080
I would argue, however, that
the university's potential

00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:48.320
is met not just in
developing the intelligence

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:52.880
of its students but also in
bringing them to understand

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:56.630
the importance of engaging
themselves in solving

00:24:56.630 --> 00:25:00.500
the problems of the world.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:05.600
The great political and
social changes around us

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:09.080
are creating
opportunities for service

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:12.440
that promise to be deeply
rewarding to persons

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:15.440
with the engaged
intelligence to be

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:18.980
successful at important
but difficult work.

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:26.000
Let me take one example to
illustrate the challenging

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:29.780
encounters to which
today's graduates might

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:33.680
apply their intelligence.

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:37.510
Tajikistan is a
mountainous country

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:44.635
in Central Asia of five million
people, more than 90% Muslim.

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:50.640
As a republic in the
former Soviet Union

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:55.150
bordering China
and Afghanistan, it

00:25:55.150 --> 00:26:02.350
had a strategic importance that
dwarfed its natural resources.

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:04.510
The Soviet Union,
therefore, invested heavily

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:08.410
in Tajikistan, building
roads and power stations,

00:26:08.410 --> 00:26:11.470
supplementing food
supplies and equipment,

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:15.730
developing the education
and health systems.

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:19.840
The result was a highly
educated, sophisticated,

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:23.170
but largely rural
population that

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:27.010
managed its affairs well at
home by the rules of the game

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:32.410
at that time and provided
well-developed human resources

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:34.910
for export to other parts
of the Soviet Union.

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:40.350
With the fall of
the Soviet Union,

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:44.550
things changed for Tajikistan.

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:50.010
Subsidies, which had provided
most of the republic's budget,

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:54.360
and for the remoter parts,
80% of the food supply,

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:57.600
were cut off.

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:03.480
The result has been hunger,
shortages of fuel and clothing,

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:05.700
and deep uncertainty
about the future.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:14.210
Long suppressed ethnic tensions
between indigenous tajiks,

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:18.110
neighboring Uzbeks and Kyrgyz,
and immigrant Russians,

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:21.080
amongst others,
became more evident

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:24.260
as groups jostled for
political and economic control.

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:30.260
Religion emerged
from private houses,

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:33.020
where it had been practiced
covertly for 60 years,

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:37.020
to become a manifest force.

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:41.480
Tajikistan has become
the focus of one

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:45.710
of the most interesting
encounters of the day.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:49.130
It is here and in
other Central Asian

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:57.800
republics that the three great
cultures encounter each other--

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:05.570
the communist world, the Muslim
world, and the Western world.

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:08.270
It is here that
those three cultures

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:13.490
could forge a success that
would contrast starkly

00:28:13.490 --> 00:28:15.740
with the brutal
failure in Bosnia.

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:21.920
The result of the
encounter in Tajikistan

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:26.120
may determine much about the way
history unfolds over the coming

00:28:26.120 --> 00:28:28.430
decades.

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:31.460
So it is worth thinking
a bit about the stance

00:28:31.460 --> 00:28:33.230
that each of these
cultures might

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:39.200
take in preparing
for this encounter.

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:42.800
That thought might
lead one to ask

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:46.430
what it would take for
this or any encounter

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:50.280
to be constructive.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:57.390
I suggest that there are four
prerequisites for success.

00:28:57.390 --> 00:29:00.690
For each of the
cultures, the result

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:07.740
should first draw on
its strengths and second

00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:11.900
be consistent with its goals.

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:16.980
Third, the result should be
a sustainable improvement

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:19.690
in the current situation.

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:23.145
And fourth, the transition
should be humane.

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:29.440
Each of these three
cultures has something

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:34.210
to bring to the solution of
the problems of Tajikistan.

00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:37.840
The West has many strengths.

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:42.400
But prominent among them
are science and democracy

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:46.270
with their public mechanisms
for self-correction

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:50.590
and also private institutions,
liberal economics,

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:53.665
and their recognition of
fundamental human rights.

00:29:56.320 --> 00:30:01.990
The Muslim world offers deep
roots in a system of values,

00:30:01.990 --> 00:30:07.840
emphasizing service, charity, a
sense of common responsibility,

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:11.530
and denying what it sees
to be the false dichotomy

00:30:11.530 --> 00:30:14.050
between religious
and secular lives.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:22.800
The ex-communist world,
although it failed economically,

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:26.280
made important investments
in social welfare

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:30.090
with particular emphasis
on the status of women

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:34.285
and was able to achieve in
Tajikistan impressive social

00:30:34.285 --> 00:30:34.785
cohesion.

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:46.980
Just how to combine
these competencies,

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:52.180
to solve Tajikistan's
problems, is not clear.

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:55.570
But if the outcome
is to be sustainable,

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:58.860
it seems necessary to
concentrate resources

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:02.850
on the development of
private institutions,

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:06.450
of accountable
public institutions,

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:10.390
and of human potential.

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:13.870
But how to get
from here to there

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:17.890
without inflicting cruel damage
on the people already buffeted

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:21.590
by shortages and change?

00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:23.800
Again, the way is
not entirely clear.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:27.670
But one should strive to
retain the powerful ties

00:31:27.670 --> 00:31:31.630
of mutual support
that in different ways

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:39.080
bind individuals together in
Muslim and communist societies.

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:42.060
And one should see that the
impressive gains in health

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:45.770
and education are not
lost in the transition,

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:48.900
for it would be
unconscionable to allow,

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:52.680
for example, the
equality of men and women

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:55.500
that has been achieved in
Tajikistan over the last 60

00:31:55.500 --> 00:32:00.460
years to be erased in the
transition to a market economy.

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:07.140
These are the
prerequisites that I

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:11.040
hope the representatives of
these three important cultures

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:14.310
will keep in mind as they
have their own encounter

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:15.510
over Tajikistan.

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.780
If the encounter of the
Muslim world, the West,

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:26.820
and the ex-communist world takes
account of the need for each

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:34.230
to draw on its own strengths,
to be consistent with its goals,

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:39.450
to strive for a sustainable,
improved outcome,

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:42.630
and to ensure a
humane transition,

00:32:42.630 --> 00:32:48.660
then the encounter have been as
successful as it is important.

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:50.640
Indeed, the importance
of Tajikistan

00:32:50.640 --> 00:32:54.810
has, if anything
increased in recent years

00:32:54.810 --> 00:32:57.450
as events in
neighboring countries

00:32:57.450 --> 00:32:59.070
continue to remind us.

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:06.030
Turning back to
today's graduates,

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:09.900
I hope that these four
prerequisites applied equally

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:12.635
to the encounter that you
are just completing with MIT.

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:19.330
Knowing the quality of
faculty and students here,

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:21.090
I have no doubt
that the encounter

00:33:21.090 --> 00:33:23.970
between you and the
faculty has drawn

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:27.360
on your respective strengths.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:32.040
I hope each of you kept
consistent with your goals,

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.620
even as they may have
evolved over your time here.

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:40.620
The quality and
sustainability of the outcome

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:45.430
will be determined over
the course of your lives.

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:48.600
But reading the smiles
amongst the graduates,

00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:53.540
I judge that the transition,
your time at MIT,

00:33:53.540 --> 00:33:56.580
must have been tolerably humane.

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:03.510
In conclusion, I would recall
the words of former MIT

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:10.270
President James
Killian Jr, quote,

00:34:10.270 --> 00:34:15.580
"We need better linkages
between science and humanities

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:20.050
with the object of fusing the
two into a broad humanism that

00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:22.719
rests upon both science
and the liberal arts

00:34:22.719 --> 00:34:26.080
and that does not weaken either.

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:30.460
We need bifocal vision to thread
our way among the problems

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:31.765
of modern society," unquote.

00:34:34.389 --> 00:34:39.070
That need to use the power
of complementary academic

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:43.570
disciplines remains true today.

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:48.280
What is now clear
is the need also

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:51.290
to draw on the wisdom
of different cultures

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:54.100
in solving those problems.

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:57.940
Thank you, and please
accept my best wishes

00:34:57.940 --> 00:35:00.970
for a lifetime of
constructive encounters.

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:01.810
Thank you.

00:35:01.810 --> 00:35:04.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:33.610
GRAY: Your highness,
thank you very

00:35:33.610 --> 00:35:38.050
much for your insightful
and challenging comments

00:35:38.050 --> 00:35:40.960
on encounters
human and cultural.

00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:45.580
And thank you so much for
gracing this commencement 36

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:48.490
years after you might have
been at another commencement

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:50.645
had your grandfather
not intervened.

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:57.290
Now Mr. Caryl Brown, president
of the Graduate Student

00:35:57.290 --> 00:35:59.720
Council, will give
a salute to MIT

00:35:59.720 --> 00:36:02.090
from the graduate student body.

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:06.470
Following this, Ms. Ann Chen,
president of the senior class,

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:09.860
will present the class
gift to President Vest.

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:13.460
After Ms. Chen's
presentation, president Vest

00:36:13.460 --> 00:36:16.509
will deliver his charge
to the graduates.

00:36:27.990 --> 00:36:30.330
BROWN: Thank you Dr. Gray.

00:36:30.330 --> 00:36:33.900
In presenting the graduate
students' salute to MIT,

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:36.120
I want to speak in
terms to which students

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:38.550
can most easily relate.

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:39.960
I don't watch Star Trek--

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:42.000
The Next Generation,
so I'll employ

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:45.420
another familiar approach
modeled after Dave Letterman's

00:36:45.420 --> 00:36:47.980
Top 10 List.

00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:51.990
So without further delay, the
top five reasons for students

00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:54.540
to salute MIT.

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:58.080
Number five-- only
at MIT can you

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:04.000
experience the joy and thrill
of turning in a 200-pound thesis

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:05.460
for a quarter ounce receipt.

00:37:05.460 --> 00:37:07.890
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:11.010
Number four-- MIT
supports an approach

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:14.280
to learning and research
that is laid back,

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:15.960
relaxed, and carefree.

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:17.708
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:24.090
Number three-- MIT continually
strives to break down barriers

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:26.730
and push limits in all areas--

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:35.050
technology, science, diversity,
and tuition cost structures.

00:37:35.050 --> 00:37:42.400
Number two-- our campus police
patrols the lows and highs

00:37:42.400 --> 00:37:47.805
of this wonderful campus
on which we work and live.

00:37:47.805 --> 00:37:50.715
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:52.660 --> 00:37:55.820
And the number-one
reason to salute MIT--

00:37:55.820 --> 00:37:59.590
we are the premier engineering
and science institution

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:03.610
in the world, not to
mention Sloan is now

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:06.940
ranked ahead of that little
business school up the river.

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:09.868
[CHEERING]

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:19.400
Graduates, when times
get tough out there,

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:23.720
just remember all of the
truly rewarding experiences

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:27.230
and relationships you
have had while at MIT.

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:28.850
They will serve you well.

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:30.890
Congratulations and
good luck always.

00:38:30.890 --> 00:38:33.785
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:47.500 --> 00:38:51.190
CHEN: I remember the last time
all of us, the class of 1994,

00:38:51.190 --> 00:38:52.600
were assembled together.

00:38:52.600 --> 00:38:55.040
It was in Kresge Auditorium
during [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:55.040 --> 00:38:56.470
of our freshman year.

00:38:56.470 --> 00:38:58.690
Former MIT President
Paul Gray told

00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:01.540
us to look at the
people seated around us.

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:03.750
He then told us one
out of five of us

00:39:03.750 --> 00:39:05.890
wouldn't graduate in four years.

00:39:05.890 --> 00:39:08.364
You may have been worried
that you wouldn't graduate.

00:39:08.364 --> 00:39:10.780
Or you could have been wondering
which one of your friends

00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:12.170
wouldn't graduate.

00:39:12.170 --> 00:39:15.770
But I think President Gray made
an impression on most of us.

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:17.290
Today, all of us have made it.

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:19.690
And we should be very proud.

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:23.230
When we entered MIT,
we were a unique class.

00:39:23.230 --> 00:39:26.110
We were supposed to be the
smartest class to enter,

00:39:26.110 --> 00:39:28.750
since we had the
highest SAT average.

00:39:28.750 --> 00:39:31.240
We were the first to
experience pass-fail,

00:39:31.240 --> 00:39:34.570
with C being passing and not D.

00:39:34.570 --> 00:39:38.920
During our four years at MIT,
we witnessed many changes.

00:39:38.920 --> 00:39:42.640
MIT became ranked number four
in US News and World Report,

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:45.610
ahead of both
Caltech and Stanford.

00:39:45.610 --> 00:39:50.260
We felt the disappearance of
a mandatory $1,500 meal plan.

00:39:50.260 --> 00:39:53.230
We saw the emergence
of two new sororities

00:39:53.230 --> 00:39:55.360
and one new fraternity.

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:58.810
We faced the possibility
of getting rid of IAP,

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:02.090
but instead we lengthened
it to a full four weeks.

00:40:02.090 --> 00:40:06.040
One safe ride van turned
into two and then four vans.

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:10.150
Our meal plan IDs and dorm keys
merged into student services

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:11.300
cards.

00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:14.020
We also observed the
Persian Gulf War.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:16.390
We witnessed the
abolishment of apartheid

00:40:16.390 --> 00:40:20.110
and the election of South
Africa's first black president.

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:23.560
We saw the tearing down
of the Berlin Wall.

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:26.920
We experienced the breakup
of the Soviet Union.

00:40:26.920 --> 00:40:30.980
All of these changes
suggest that change is good

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:33.580
and that change is improvement.

00:40:33.580 --> 00:40:36.700
We all have the ability to
make changes, whether globally,

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:38.270
nationally, or locally.

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:40.360
And I believe we have a
certain responsibility

00:40:40.360 --> 00:40:42.470
to make a difference.

00:40:42.470 --> 00:40:46.780
Our first change as an alumni
class is our senior class gift.

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:50.140
All of us have benefited
from past alumni class gifts,

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:53.230
whether they are renovated
classrooms or financial aid

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:54.520
or something else.

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:58.705
And now future classes
will benefit from our gift.

00:40:58.705 --> 00:41:04.270
As a class, we have already
raised $43,000 for MIT.

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:07.510
I am pleased to announce
that 24% of our class

00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:10.290
has participated in
this year's senior gift,

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:14.770
and 87% of those pledges
are over a five-year period.

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:16.450
The majority of
our classmates have

00:41:16.450 --> 00:41:20.290
chosen to have their gift help
fund the senior project, which

00:41:20.290 --> 00:41:23.180
involves renovating and
expanding the Stratton Student

00:41:23.180 --> 00:41:26.890
Center information desk and
adding to it an enclosed box

00:41:26.890 --> 00:41:28.000
office.

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:30.970
I envision the MIT ticket
and information source

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:34.450
becoming a center where
the MIT community can track

00:41:34.450 --> 00:41:37.910
major Institute,
community, national,

00:41:37.910 --> 00:41:41.570
and international happenings
through a television monitor,

00:41:41.570 --> 00:41:44.060
events board, and database.

00:41:44.060 --> 00:41:46.330
In addition, students
will be able to pick up

00:41:46.330 --> 00:41:49.300
important forms, reserve
[INAUDIBLE] poster and Lobby 10

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:54.000
space, and buy tickets to MIT
events all in the same place.

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:57.820
Class of 1994 graduates, we have
made a worthwhile difference.

00:41:57.820 --> 00:41:58.778
Thank you.

00:41:58.778 --> 00:42:01.646
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:09.310 --> 00:42:12.130
VEST: Thank you, Ms. Chen.

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:16.930
It is a moving experience
to receive for the Institute

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:20.480
a gift from the class
at a moment like this,

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:23.530
especially when we think about
all we've put you through.

00:42:23.530 --> 00:42:26.230
We appreciate it very much.

00:42:26.230 --> 00:42:29.740
Your gift of new information
booth in the student center

00:42:29.740 --> 00:42:32.580
is certainly fitting
for these times.

00:42:32.580 --> 00:42:34.840
And I'm sure your
class will in many ways

00:42:34.840 --> 00:42:38.890
be racing ahead of most of us
down the emerging information

00:42:38.890 --> 00:42:39.890
highway.

00:42:39.890 --> 00:42:41.950
And we are very
grateful for the lift

00:42:41.950 --> 00:42:44.920
that this gift will give to all
the students who follow you.

00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:45.880
Thank you very much.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:52.180
Once again, we are assembled in
Killian Court, the great court

00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:56.920
of MIT, to celebrate
accomplishment, heritage,

00:42:56.920 --> 00:42:58.990
and passage.

00:42:58.990 --> 00:43:01.280
It may, perhaps, seem
odd that a community

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:04.840
so dedicated to the
future, so permeated

00:43:04.840 --> 00:43:08.620
by scientific objectivity,
comes together wearing

00:43:08.620 --> 00:43:12.130
strange and colorful
medieval regalia.

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:14.230
But indeed, it is
fitting and seemingly

00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:19.600
fulfilling of deep human needs
that such rituals take place.

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:24.040
They remind us of the continuity
through the ages of discovery

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:28.600
and learning, of our role in
an unbroken centuries-old chain

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:31.750
of human accomplishment,
achievements of mind

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:33.580
and of spirit.

00:43:33.580 --> 00:43:37.300
But above all, it celebrates
your accomplishments

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:40.010
during your student years.

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:41.920
This is not to say that
you have accomplished

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:43.780
the remarkable
feat of graduating

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:46.540
from MIT on your own, however.

00:43:46.540 --> 00:43:50.770
We are surrounded today by
parents, family, friends,

00:43:50.770 --> 00:43:54.550
spouses, and children who have
supported and sustained you

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:56.380
through the years.

00:43:56.380 --> 00:44:00.010
You will recognize them today
by their smiles, brought

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:03.310
about by their great pride
in your accomplishment

00:44:03.310 --> 00:44:08.080
and no doubt by a sense of great
relief to their bank accounts.

00:44:08.080 --> 00:44:10.360
Let us then express
our appreciation

00:44:10.360 --> 00:44:12.400
to all who have come
to Cambridge today

00:44:12.400 --> 00:44:15.250
to join in this
commencement ceremony.

00:44:15.250 --> 00:44:18.910
Will you, the graduates, please
rise, turn to your audience,

00:44:18.910 --> 00:44:22.392
and give them the applause they
so deeply and richly deserve.

00:44:22.392 --> 00:44:25.344
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:44.060 --> 00:44:47.120
It is also especially
wonderful to see

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:50.240
the babies and small children
who come to see their mothers

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:51.980
and fathers graduate.

00:44:51.980 --> 00:44:53.900
They, too, are welcome.

00:44:53.900 --> 00:44:57.680
And as this ceremony stretches
onward, I give each of them

00:44:57.680 --> 00:44:59.990
special presidential
approval to comment

00:44:59.990 --> 00:45:04.040
upon the proceedings at any time
and in any manner they see fit.

00:45:06.590 --> 00:45:09.740
In delivering this
charge, my task

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:13.160
should be to set before you
great principles by which

00:45:13.160 --> 00:45:14.930
to live.

00:45:14.930 --> 00:45:18.740
Yet in this age of relativism,
as I ponder just which

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:22.020
principles to
disclose to you today,

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:26.030
I recall the statement of
no less a great intellect

00:45:26.030 --> 00:45:30.800
than Groucho Marx, who once
said with great conviction,

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:33.734
these are my principles.

00:45:33.734 --> 00:45:35.900
And you don't like them, I
have got some other ones.

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:37.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:37.770 --> 00:45:40.970
So on second thought, I decided
I would bypass the wisdom

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:42.770
and just remind you
of an experience

00:45:42.770 --> 00:45:45.680
that we have shared together.

00:45:45.680 --> 00:45:50.090
Some of you will recall that
this class is also my class.

00:45:50.090 --> 00:45:54.860
On August 31, 1990, we came
together for the first time,

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:59.750
you as MIT's new freshman class,
and I as MIT's new freshman

00:45:59.750 --> 00:46:01.310
president.

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:04.790
That moment, which Ann
referred to a moment ago,

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:07.280
standing before 1,100
of the brightest

00:46:07.280 --> 00:46:10.490
and most creative young men and
women from around this nation

00:46:10.490 --> 00:46:14.480
and around the world, gathered
together in Kresge Auditorium

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:18.110
to begin their education
at MIT, was surely

00:46:18.110 --> 00:46:21.560
one of the most
exhilarating of my life.

00:46:21.560 --> 00:46:24.230
On that day, we were all
proud to have been selected

00:46:24.230 --> 00:46:26.810
to join the ranks of MIT.

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:28.850
We shared the
exhilaration of becoming

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:32.510
part of its intellectual
and creative atmosphere.

00:46:32.510 --> 00:46:34.520
We were confident
and eager to get on

00:46:34.520 --> 00:46:36.920
with the year's activities.

00:46:36.920 --> 00:46:40.610
But to tell the truth, we also
were a little apprehensive

00:46:40.610 --> 00:46:44.420
about the less certain
aspects of what lay ahead.

00:46:44.420 --> 00:46:46.130
Nonetheless, we knew
that we were all

00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:49.880
embarking on the next
stage of a great adventure.

00:46:49.880 --> 00:46:52.520
I said to you that day that
I hoped you would accept me

00:46:52.520 --> 00:46:55.190
as a member of your class and
trusted that you would allow

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:57.860
me to live and work among you.

00:46:57.860 --> 00:47:01.820
I also noted that I looked
forward to one other occasion

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:04.890
when I would stand before
you in a formal setting

00:47:04.890 --> 00:47:06.560
and that that
would be in Killian

00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:11.690
Court at your graduation
in the spring of 1994.

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:15.112
Well, we made it,
and here we are.

00:47:15.112 --> 00:47:17.096
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:19.080 --> 00:47:23.910
For all of us, these four
years have been intense.

00:47:23.910 --> 00:47:26.970
They seem to have passed
in the twinkling of an eye

00:47:26.970 --> 00:47:30.330
and also to have
lasted a lifetime.

00:47:30.330 --> 00:47:34.320
We moved through changes in our
internal and external worlds

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:36.780
that were beyond our imagining.

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:39.770
We encountered new
ideas and new people.

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:43.530
We were enriched by what we
learned in these encounters.

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:46.230
Above all, we learned to
couple intellectual rigor

00:47:46.230 --> 00:47:49.930
and discipline with
creativity and innovation.

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:51.780
And we gained the
self-confidence

00:47:51.780 --> 00:47:55.660
to approach virtually any
challenge that may lie ahead.

00:47:55.660 --> 00:47:59.640
And the challenges
before us are great.

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:02.620
His highness, by his
presence here today

00:48:02.620 --> 00:48:05.190
and in the thoughts
he has shared with us,

00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:09.810
reminded each of us just how
great these challenges are.

00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:12.840
We live and work in a rapidly
changing and increasingly

00:48:12.840 --> 00:48:15.900
interdependent world society.

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:19.620
It is a world in which new
balances must be struck.

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:22.440
We must resolve the
tensions between competition

00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:27.870
and cooperation, between
fragmentation and integration,

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:30.720
between nation and world.

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:33.090
This will take some learning.

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:36.210
We understand well the
value of competition.

00:48:36.210 --> 00:48:40.530
We know the value of the rugged
individual and the iconoclast.

00:48:40.530 --> 00:48:43.320
But we are just beginning
to learn the value

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:46.260
of cooperation and of teamwork.

00:48:46.260 --> 00:48:49.260
Economically,
socially, politically--

00:48:49.260 --> 00:48:52.380
if you want to shape a
vibrant and just future,

00:48:52.380 --> 00:48:57.120
you must learn to cooperate
as well as to compete.

00:48:57.120 --> 00:48:59.160
The message of
cooperation is simple,

00:48:59.160 --> 00:49:02.160
but its implementation
is not, for we

00:49:02.160 --> 00:49:06.510
seem to have fragmented along
every conceivable fault line,

00:49:06.510 --> 00:49:10.830
fragmented by intellectual
discipline, fragmented by race,

00:49:10.830 --> 00:49:14.910
fragmented by gender,
fragmented by geography.

00:49:14.910 --> 00:49:18.480
In the international arena,
we have stepped back at last

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:20.745
from the superpower standoff.

00:49:20.745 --> 00:49:23.520
But we find peace and
stability challenged

00:49:23.520 --> 00:49:28.110
by the unleashed forces of
nationalism, ethnic hatred,

00:49:28.110 --> 00:49:30.210
and local warfare.

00:49:30.210 --> 00:49:33.150
With every such
division, we lose

00:49:33.150 --> 00:49:36.780
more of a sense of
our common humanity.

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:40.560
It will not be easy to resolve
our differences and tensions.

00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:42.210
But if we are to
build the future we

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:44.790
want for ourselves
and our children,

00:49:44.790 --> 00:49:47.190
we must build it together.

00:49:47.190 --> 00:49:50.110
We must have a sense
of common purpose.

00:49:50.110 --> 00:49:54.010
We must have an integrated,
inclusive view of history.

00:49:54.010 --> 00:49:55.710
We must have community.

00:49:55.710 --> 00:49:58.110
We must have mutual respect.

00:49:58.110 --> 00:50:01.890
We must hold common values
at the deepest levels.

00:50:01.890 --> 00:50:05.430
I believe this is
possible because I believe

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:09.840
that the true historical
trend is one of communication,

00:50:09.840 --> 00:50:13.380
interaction, and cooperation.

00:50:13.380 --> 00:50:16.110
Witness the people
of South Africa

00:50:16.110 --> 00:50:18.930
who have at last placed
apartheid behind them

00:50:18.930 --> 00:50:22.380
and set off on a
brave, new path.

00:50:22.380 --> 00:50:26.100
I do not suggest that we
sweep aside all differences.

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:30.540
Quite the contrary, we thrive
on differences of experience,

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:33.240
culture, and perspective.

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:37.740
As Alfred North Whitehead said
in his 1925 lectures on science

00:50:37.740 --> 00:50:41.520
in the modern world,
quote, "Other nations

00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:44.220
of different habits
are not enemies.

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:47.070
They are godsends."

00:50:47.070 --> 00:50:50.130
This is true whether we speak
of society's professions

00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:52.410
or single institutions.

00:50:52.410 --> 00:50:55.050
The electrical engineer
and the mechanical engineer

00:50:55.050 --> 00:50:57.240
are able to build
systems together

00:50:57.240 --> 00:50:59.670
which neither can build alone.

00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:03.230
Men and women together create
a balanced discourse and world

00:51:03.230 --> 00:51:04.560
view.

00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:07.230
Black and white, brown
and yellow, red and tan

00:51:07.230 --> 00:51:11.790
create a campus and a nation
far more meaningful and creative

00:51:11.790 --> 00:51:14.460
than any could alone.

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:17.910
As you shape the future,
you must respect and cherish

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:23.280
differences, but you must build
common purpose and values.

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:27.330
MIT is an American
institution, and each of you

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:29.460
is a citizen of some nation.

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:33.780
For most of you, that nation is
the United States of America.

00:51:33.780 --> 00:51:37.470
But MIT is also an
institution of the world.

00:51:37.470 --> 00:51:40.290
And its greatness
derives in large measure

00:51:40.290 --> 00:51:42.840
from its cosmopolitan
nature and connections

00:51:42.840 --> 00:51:44.760
throughout the world.

00:51:44.760 --> 00:51:47.370
The same must be true of you.

00:51:47.370 --> 00:51:50.140
Be proud and committed
to your nations,

00:51:50.140 --> 00:51:52.440
but also look beyond them.

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:54.840
To shape a future
of greatness, you

00:51:54.840 --> 00:51:58.100
must be citizens of the world.

00:51:58.100 --> 00:52:01.080
And though this world seems
increasingly less predictable

00:52:01.080 --> 00:52:06.610
and less stable, the opportunity
to discover, learn, grow,

00:52:06.610 --> 00:52:10.260
and serve has never
been so great.

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:13.200
I am optimistic that
we and especially you

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:16.380
will make the most
of this opportunity.

00:52:16.380 --> 00:52:18.560
Why am I optimistic?

00:52:18.560 --> 00:52:22.310
We need only look
around ourselves today.

00:52:22.310 --> 00:52:24.740
Our faculty and our
students are the reason

00:52:24.740 --> 00:52:27.320
for this confidence
and this hope,

00:52:27.320 --> 00:52:31.790
because MIT is a place that
helps to shape the future.

00:52:31.790 --> 00:52:35.090
Because of our
faculty, MIT is a place

00:52:35.090 --> 00:52:40.400
where science advances, where
art thrives, where technology

00:52:40.400 --> 00:52:44.720
extends our reach, where thought
leads to new order, where

00:52:44.720 --> 00:52:46.610
new modes of
teaching and learning

00:52:46.610 --> 00:52:49.540
are invented and put to use.

00:52:49.540 --> 00:52:53.516
But the still deeper
reason for optimism is you.

00:52:53.516 --> 00:52:56.630
You are unmatched in your
talent, your education,

00:52:56.630 --> 00:53:00.470
your sense of service, your
entrepreneurial spirit,

00:53:00.470 --> 00:53:02.690
and your basic goodness.

00:53:02.690 --> 00:53:05.960
Beyond that, you have had
the remarkable experience

00:53:05.960 --> 00:53:09.290
of living and learning
in a truly international,

00:53:09.290 --> 00:53:11.560
multicultural community.

00:53:11.560 --> 00:53:14.000
And all the while, you have
been setting and meeting

00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:16.880
your own highest standards.

00:53:16.880 --> 00:53:21.950
With such a legacy, you give us
all great hope for the future.

00:53:21.950 --> 00:53:24.080
You will not fail us.

00:53:24.080 --> 00:53:26.600
You will not fail that future.

00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:31.724
Men and women of MIT, I wish
you Godspeed and the very best

00:53:31.724 --> 00:53:32.390
of good fortune.

00:53:32.390 --> 00:53:35.354
[APPLAUSE]

