[APPLAUSE]

02:30:18.360 --> 02:30:23.820
GRAY: I speak now particularly
to members of the class of 1944

02:30:23.820 --> 02:30:26.400
who were part of the procession
and who are sitting here

02:30:26.400 --> 02:30:28.800
to the left of the stage.

02:30:28.800 --> 02:30:31.170
In accordance with
our traditions,

02:30:31.170 --> 02:30:33.060
members of the
50th reunion class

02:30:33.060 --> 02:30:35.730
are participating
in the ceremonies.

02:30:35.730 --> 02:30:39.710
This class, the class of
1944, entered the Institute

02:30:39.710 --> 02:30:43.170
before the United States
entered World War II

02:30:43.170 --> 02:30:45.750
and may have had the
expectation not held

02:30:45.750 --> 02:30:49.380
by later wartime classes
of a normal completion

02:30:49.380 --> 02:30:52.110
of their undergraduate degrees.

02:30:52.110 --> 02:30:56.640
For most members of the class
of 1944, this was not to be.

02:30:56.640 --> 02:30:59.100
The records of the
Institute indicate

02:30:59.100 --> 02:31:03.660
that of all the wartime classes
at MIT, the class of 1944

02:31:03.660 --> 02:31:06.710
may well have experienced
the greatest disruption.

02:31:06.710 --> 02:31:10.170
Most who entered with this class
did not receive their diplomas

02:31:10.170 --> 02:31:12.970
until after the war, if at all.

02:31:12.970 --> 02:31:15.880
Many did not return
from the war.

02:31:15.880 --> 02:31:19.870
Today as you return to
Tech for your 50th reunion,

02:31:19.870 --> 02:31:22.470
we extend a special
welcome to you.

02:31:22.470 --> 02:31:24.540
May your participation
in these commencement

02:31:24.540 --> 02:31:28.980
ceremonies recognize and
reaffirm your special place

02:31:28.980 --> 02:31:32.178
in the history
and family of MIT.

02:31:32.178 --> 02:31:34.638
[APPLAUSE]

02:31:53.830 --> 02:31:56.200
It is now my pleasure to
introduce Mr. Richard A.

02:31:56.200 --> 02:31:59.620
Jacobs, the Chief Marshal,
who will greet the graduates.

02:31:59.620 --> 02:32:03.490
Mr. Jacobs is a member
of the class of 1956

02:32:03.490 --> 02:32:05.410
and is currently
serving as president

02:32:05.410 --> 02:32:07.910
of the MIT Association
of Alumni and Alumnae.

02:32:14.280 --> 02:32:16.830
JACOBS: Thank you, Dr. Gray.

02:32:16.830 --> 02:32:21.150
May 27 is one of the
longest days in the year--

02:32:21.150 --> 02:32:26.730
14 hours and 58 minutes, dawn
to dusk, from today's newspaper.

02:32:26.730 --> 02:32:29.785
I do not intend to
make it any longer.

02:32:29.785 --> 02:32:32.150
[CHEERING]

02:32:34.520 --> 02:32:39.060
Let me leave you with one more
simple equation to consider,

02:32:39.060 --> 02:32:44.900
adopt, and use in your
continuing quest for knowledge.

02:32:44.900 --> 02:32:50.930
Lombardi's axiom states, "The
quality of a person's life

02:32:50.930 --> 02:32:53.780
is directly proportional
to their commitment

02:32:53.780 --> 02:32:55.250
to excellence."

02:32:55.250 --> 02:32:59.330
Today, you take a major
step in that commitment.

02:32:59.330 --> 02:33:01.910
Remember this day.

02:33:01.910 --> 02:33:05.510
Look once more around
this great court.

02:33:05.510 --> 02:33:07.940
It belongs to you.

02:33:07.940 --> 02:33:09.860
Take pride.

02:33:09.860 --> 02:33:13.850
You are a most uncommon group.

02:33:13.850 --> 02:33:16.010
Keep in contact.

02:33:16.010 --> 02:33:18.560
Come back often.

02:33:18.560 --> 02:33:21.830
Help others to achieve what
you have accomplished and will

02:33:21.830 --> 02:33:25.410
realize later in life.

02:33:25.410 --> 02:33:28.820
Now join me and the
distinguished members

02:33:28.820 --> 02:33:35.930
of the 50th and 25th reunion
classes of 1944 and 1969

02:33:35.930 --> 02:33:39.830
as we applaud today's
graduates and welcome

02:33:39.830 --> 02:33:43.940
into that most select
company of men and women

02:33:43.940 --> 02:33:47.734
who are MIT's
alumni and alumnae.

02:33:47.734 --> 02:33:49.198
Thank you.

02:33:49.198 --> 02:33:51.638
[APPLAUSE]

02:34:03.370 --> 02:34:06.010
GRAY: The 128th
Commencement Exercises

02:34:06.010 --> 02:34:08.440
of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

02:34:08.440 --> 02:34:10.400
are now concluded.

02:34:10.400 --> 02:34:13.840
[CHEERING]

02:34:13.840 --> 02:34:16.330
Please join now the
Backlogs Quartet,

02:34:16.330 --> 02:34:18.700
a splinter group of
former Logarithms,

02:34:18.700 --> 02:34:20.410
in singing the school song.

02:34:20.410 --> 02:34:23.070
The stage assembly and the
graduates will please rise.

02:34:27.070 --> 02:34:29.566
[MUSIC - "ARISE ALL YE OF MIT"]

02:34:47.530 --> 02:34:57.295
BACKLOGS QUARTET: Arise all ye
of MIT, in loyal fellowship.

02:35:01.063 --> 02:35:13.120
The future beckons unto ye
and life is full and rich.

02:35:13.120 --> 02:35:19.766
Arise and raise
your glass on high.

02:35:19.766 --> 02:35:28.654
Tonight shall ever
be a memory that

02:35:28.654 --> 02:35:34.390
will never die for ye of MIT.

02:35:37.970 --> 02:35:50.502
Thy sons and daughters, MIT,
return from far and wide.

02:35:50.502 --> 02:36:00.774
And gather here once more to
be renourished by thy side.

02:36:03.296 --> 02:36:16.100
And as we raise our glasses high
to pledge our love for thee,

02:36:16.100 --> 02:36:25.772
we join all those of days
gone by in praise of MIT.

02:36:29.840 --> 02:36:32.540
[MUSIC - "TAKE ME BACK TO TECH"]

02:36:32.540 --> 02:36:38.410
I wish that I were back again
at Tech on Boylston Street,

02:36:38.410 --> 02:36:43.460
dressed in my dinky uniform,
so dapper and so neat.

02:36:43.460 --> 02:36:46.180
I'm crazy after calculus.

02:36:46.180 --> 02:36:48.570
I never had enough.

02:36:48.570 --> 02:36:51.000
It was hard to be
dragged away so young.

02:36:51.000 --> 02:36:53.560
It was horribly, awfully tough.

02:36:53.560 --> 02:36:58.270
Hurrah for technology,
'ology, 'ology, oh.

02:36:58.270 --> 02:37:02.820
Glorious old technology,
'ology, 'ology, oh.

02:37:02.820 --> 02:37:04.715
Back in the days that
were free from care

02:37:04.715 --> 02:37:09.620
in the 'ology varsity shop, with
nothing to do but analyze air

02:37:09.620 --> 02:37:11.130
in anemometrical top.

02:37:11.130 --> 02:37:15.600
Or the differentiation of
the trigonometric powers,

02:37:15.600 --> 02:37:17.700
the constant pi
that made me sigh,

02:37:17.700 --> 02:37:20.134
in those happy days of ours.

02:37:20.134 --> 02:37:24.150
Hurrah for technology,
'ology, ology, oh.

02:37:24.150 --> 02:37:28.380
Glorious old technology,
'ology, 'ology, oh.

02:37:28.380 --> 02:37:31.780
Take me back on a special train
to that glorious Institute.

02:37:31.780 --> 02:37:35.060
I yearn for the inspiration
of a technological toot.

02:37:35.060 --> 02:37:38.700
I'd shun the quizzical physical
prof, the chapel, and all that,

02:37:38.700 --> 02:37:41.910
but how I'd love to go
again on a scientific bat.

02:37:41.910 --> 02:37:45.450
Hurrah for technology,
'ology, 'ology, oh.

02:37:45.450 --> 02:37:48.315
Glorious old technology,
'ology, 'ology, oh.

02:37:51.940 --> 02:37:58.150
M-A-S-S-A-C-H-U-S-E-T-T-S
I-N-S-T-I-T-U-T-E-O-F-T-E

02:37:58.150 --> 02:38:03.480
